{"event":"trap-result","runId":"briefing-proof3-2026-11-16/fork-0/branch-3","trapId":"briefing-proof3-2026-11-16/fork-0/branch-3/trap-0","ordinal":0,"result":{"ok":true,"error":"","id":"2611.08669","title":"ChainFlow: Few-Step Latent Flow Matching for Speculative Decoding","authors":"Selim Aktas , Yusuf Erdem Erdem , Ahmed Zeer , Himmet Toprak Kesgin , Muzaffer Kaan Yuce , Mehmet Fatih Amasyali","submitted":"5 Nov 2026","abstract":"Speculative decoding has become a standard way to accelerate LLM inference: a lightweight drafter proposes tokens that the target verifies in one parallel pass. Existing drafters face trade-offs that limits their speedups: autoregressive drafters generate tokens one at a time, so drafting cost grows with draft length, while diffusion drafters generate tokens in parallel but predict each position independently, ignoring inter-token dependencies. We introduce ChainFlow, a new speculative decoding paradigm and the first flow-matching drafter conditioned on the target's hidden states. ChainFlow treats drafting as a short transport over hidden states, needing one or two flow steps instead of the many denoising steps of generation from noise. A variational autoencoder compresses these states so drafting cost barely grows with target size, lightweight feedback heads restore inter-token dependencies, and tree drafting raises acceptance. On Qwen3.5 models, at matched drafter size and training data, ChainFlow outperforms EAGLE-3 and DFlash in vLLM by 39% and 11%, respectively. These gains further widen as drafters are adapted to new languages sequentially. Our ablations cover drafter design and concurrent serving, and we open-source our code and drafter models.","numbers":["On Qwen3.5 models, at matched drafter size and training data, ChainFlow outperforms EAGLE-3 and DFlash in vLLM by 39% and 11%, respectively."],"method":["We introduce ChainFlow, a new speculative decoding paradigm and the first flow-matching drafter conditioned on the target's hidden states."],"caveats":[],"topics":["diffusion","speculative-decoding"],"score":12,"fullText":true,"readChars":6042,"absUrl":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08669","htmlUrl":"https://ar5iv.org/html/2611.08669"}}
{"event":"branch-done","runId":"briefing-proof3-2026-11-16/fork-0/branch-3","parentForkId":"briefing-proof3-2026-11-16/fork-0","branch":3,"outcome":{"ok":true,"value":{"ok":true,"error":"","id":"2611.08669","title":"ChainFlow: Few-Step Latent Flow Matching for Speculative Decoding","authors":"Selim Aktas , Yusuf Erdem Erdem , Ahmed Zeer , Himmet Toprak Kesgin , Muzaffer Kaan Yuce , Mehmet Fatih Amasyali","submitted":"5 Nov 2026","abstract":"Speculative decoding has become a standard way to accelerate LLM inference: a lightweight drafter proposes tokens that the target verifies in one parallel pass. Existing drafters face trade-offs that limits their speedups: autoregressive drafters generate tokens one at a time, so drafting cost grows with draft length, while diffusion drafters generate tokens in parallel but predict each position independently, ignoring inter-token dependencies. We introduce ChainFlow, a new speculative decoding paradigm and the first flow-matching drafter conditioned on the target's hidden states. ChainFlow treats drafting as a short transport over hidden states, needing one or two flow steps instead of the many denoising steps of generation from noise. A variational autoencoder compresses these states so drafting cost barely grows with target size, lightweight feedback heads restore inter-token dependencies, and tree drafting raises acceptance. On Qwen3.5 models, at matched drafter size and training data, ChainFlow outperforms EAGLE-3 and DFlash in vLLM by 39% and 11%, respectively. These gains further widen as drafters are adapted to new languages sequentially. Our ablations cover drafter design and concurrent serving, and we open-source our code and drafter models.","numbers":["On Qwen3.5 models, at matched drafter size and training data, ChainFlow outperforms EAGLE-3 and DFlash in vLLM by 39% and 11%, respectively."],"method":["We introduce ChainFlow, a new speculative decoding paradigm and the first flow-matching drafter conditioned on the target's hidden states."],"caveats":[],"topics":["diffusion","speculative-decoding"],"score":12,"fullText":true,"readChars":6042,"absUrl":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08669","htmlUrl":"https://ar5iv.org/html/2611.08669"}}}
